== (i) d = 3, eventless: exchange^2 holonomy = identity ==
  contraction stage s = 1.0: ||holonomy - 1||_max = 2.15e-14
  contraction stage s = 0.6: ||holonomy - 1||_max = 1.16e-14
  contraction stage s = 0.3: ||holonomy - 1||_max = 7.34e-15
  -> for EVENTLESS (flat = pure-gauge) fiber transport the
     exchange-squared loop carries NO holonomy, at every stage of
     the contraction: eps^2 = 1 is FORCED in d = 3 - braid
     statistics collapses to the symmetric group (Theorem R1).

== (ii) d = 3, eventful: holonomy lives on enclosed events ==
  s = 1.00: ||holonomy - 1||_max = 1.987e+00
  s = 0.50: ||holonomy - 1||_max = 1.962e+00
  s = 0.25: ||holonomy - 1||_max = 6.988e-01
  s = 0.10: ||holonomy - 1||_max = 1.153e-01
  -> with curvature (events) present the loop holonomy is O(1) at
     full size and vanishes as the contraction empties the loop:
     exchange-squared holonomy measures ENCLOSED EVENTS, never
     statistics.  Eventless exchange-squared = 1, always.

== (iii) d = 2: the flat modulus survives ==
  flat connection on S^1: A = X dtheta, holonomy of the
  exchange-squared (winding-1) loop = exp(2 pi i X):
  ||holonomy - 1||_max = 1.025   (an ARBITRARY U(3) element)
  -> pi_1(S^1) = Z: flat connections carry a modulus; the braid
     group survives ONLY in d = 2 - Paper 9's record-anyon window
     recovered as the unique exception, now from the same theorem
     that forces eps^2 = 1 everywhere else.
